//
// loft#1229 — a keyed literal does not report its DESTINATION's nullability, so loft#1210's
// `(N-Store)` append gate stops warning about code that is correct.
//
// A keyed literal is built THROUGH its destination (loft#703), so `parse_vector` reported the
// destination VARIABLE's type as the literal's own.  For a `hash<E[k]>?` destination that made
// the constructed literal `Optional(Hash(…))`, and the append gate's collection-to-collection
// disjunct — `s_type.base().is_equal(f_type.base())` — matched it.  The gate then said *"a
// nullable `hash<E,["k"]>?` is stored into the appended value of the non-null type"* about a
// statement whose source is a freshly built literal that cannot be absent at all.
//
// `warning` is the tier that GATES a library's CI under `LOFT_DENY_WARNINGS=1`, and the cure the
// message names — discharge with `?` or `??` — does not apply, because there is nothing to
// discharge.  So this was a build failure for correct code with no action available to fix it.
//
// The VECTOR branch three lines from the fix has always constructed its type fresh, which is
// why only the keyed spelling carried this.  `.base()` is the whole change.
//
// SCORED BY THE DIAGNOSTIC COUNT, not by a value.  `tests/wrap.rs` fails a file that emits a
// warning it did not declare, so the first cell scores the absence and the `@EXPECT_WARNING`
// cell below scores that the gate still FIRES where it should — a fix that silenced the check
// outright would pass the first cell and fail the second.
//
// ⚠ The first cell's VALUE is deliberately not asserted.  `t.0 += [e]` on a tuple element is
// loft#1225's open half — it replaces the collection rather than appending to it — and this
// file is about the diagnostic, not the store.  Asserting the length here would tie a
// warning fix to a defect it does not own, and the file would start failing when loft#1225
// lands rather than when this regresses.
//
// @falsified-at: 7d49c9ad — interpret and native both: this file's cell emits the warning on
//   the control, which `tests/wrap.rs` scores as an undeclared diagnostic and fails the corpus
//   on. `make falsify` cannot render that channel — its refusal column counts errors, not
//   warnings — so the gating channel is `check_diagnostics` in `tests/wrap.rs`, measured by
//   running the corpus on both trees.
//   CHANNEL: an UNDECLARED DIAGNOSTIC — the cell emits a warning on the control which
//   `tests/wrap.rs` scores and fails the corpus on. Not the exit, not an assertion, and not a
//   channel `falsify` renders: its refusal column counts errors, so the sweep sees nothing
//   move.
//   HOLDS: every scored channel is equal on both trees, which is why the bulk row is INERT.
//   The measurement lives in the corpus runner's diagnostic check, so re-validate through
//   `tests/wrap.rs` rather than through a falsify signature.

struct E1229 { k: integer, nm: text }
struct N1229 { c: vector<integer> }

fn main() {
  // The cell: a keyed LITERAL appended to a nullable keyed collection. It must not warn — the
  // source is a construction and a construction is never absent.
  h1229: hash<E1229[k]>? = null;
  h1229[9] = E1229 { k: 9, nm: "nine" };
  t1229: (hash<E1229[k]>?, integer) = (h1229, 5);
  t1229.0 += [E1229 { k: 1, nm: "one" }];

  // The same literal into a nullable keyed LOCAL and FIELD — the two spellings that were
  // already quiet, kept so a fix that peels too much shows up here.
  l1229: hash<E1229[k]>? = [];
  l1229 += [E1229 { k: 2, nm: "two" }];
  assert(len(l1229?) == 1, "a keyed literal still appends to a nullable local");

  v1229: vector<integer>? = [];
  v1229 += [7];
  assert(len(v1229?) == 1, "and the vector spelling, which never carried this");

  n1229_real_nullable_source_still_warns();
}

// The gate must still FIRE for a real nullable SOURCE — a place read that can genuinely be
// absent, which is what `(N-Store)` is about. Without this cell, deleting the check passes.
// @EXPECT_WARNING: is stored into the appended value of the non-null type
fn n1229_real_nullable_source_still_warns() {
  s1229: vector<integer>? = [1, 2];
  d1229 = N1229 { c: [] };
  d1229.c += s1229;
  assert(len(d1229.c) == 2, "and the store still proceeds, as (N-Store) says it does");
}
